    {
        'title': 'Multi-Region Deployment and Tradeoffs',
        'content': 'Deploying applications in multiple regions can improve reliability and reduce latency for global users, but it introduces design trade-offs. In an **active-active** multi-region setup, your service runs in two or more regions and serves traffic from all of them. This yields high availability (if one region goes down, others still serve) and lower latency per user region, but requires dealing with data consistency across regions (which can be challenging due to network latency and the CAP theorem). Often, multi-region systems have to choose between strong consistency and availability; many opt for eventual consistency models for data replication. Alternatively, an **active-passive** (primary/DR) setup is simpler: one region serves until a failover is needed. It’s easier to maintain consistency (only one active DB), but failover procedures must be tested and there is downtime in a failover. Active-active demands more from your databases (multi-master or conflict resolution strategies) and your DNS/load-balancing (to route users to nearest or healthiest region). It also costs more to run duplicate capacity. A best practice is to only go multi-region active-active for critical services that require near-zero downtime or have global low-latency requirements; otherwise, regional HA (multi-zone) and a cold/warm secondary region might suffice and be simpler. The decision involves weighing user experience, business continuity needs, and system complexity.',
        'keywords': ['multi-region', 'active-active', 'disaster recovery', 'latency', 'consistency', 'failover', 'trade-offs'],
        'category': 'General',
    },
    {
        'title': 'Batch vs Streaming Data Pipelines',
        'content': 'Data processing pipelines generally come in two flavors: batch (processing data in large chunks at intervals) and streaming (processing data continuously in real-time). **Batch pipelines** (e.g., a daily ETL job) are simpler to implement when real-time results aren’t needed; they can efficiently process large volumes but introduce inherent latency (data is only up-to-date after each batch, maybe once a day or hour). **Streaming pipelines** (e.g., using Pub/Sub and Dataflow to handle events) process data as it arrives, enabling near real-time analytics or instant trigger-driven actions. The trade-offs: streaming systems are more complex (you must handle event time, out-of-order data, incremental aggregation) and typically cost more to run continuously, whereas batch jobs can be optimized to run during off-peak times and have well-defined start/stop. Some scenarios naturally fit one model (billing, reporting, backups tend to be batch; monitoring, alerting, user interactions often need streaming). Often, a hybrid approach is used: streaming for immediate insights on recent data, and batch for comprehensive reprocessing or backfilling data corrections. The key is to match the pipeline style to business requirements for data freshness, while considering cost and complexity. Modern data architectures (the “lambda architecture”) combine both to balance accuracy and latency.',
        'keywords': ['batch processing', 'stream processing', 'ETL', 'real-time', 'latency', 'data pipeline', 'trade-off'],
        'category': 'General',
    },
    {
        'title': 'Monolithic vs Microservice Architecture',
        'content': 'In software architecture, a **monolith** is a single unified application that encompasses all functionalities, whereas a **microservices** architecture splits a system into many small, independent services communicating via APIs. A monolith is simpler to develop, test, and deploy initially (one deployment contains everything), which can be advantageous for small teams or straightforward domains. However, as the application grows, monoliths can become unwieldy – deploys of one feature require re-deploying the entire app, scaling is one-dimensional (scale everything together), and different parts of the system are tightly coupled. Microservices, by contrast, isolate functionality into separate services (for example, billing, user profile, recommendation engine each as a service). This allows each service to be scaled, updated, and deployed independently, possibly even with different tech stacks. The trade-off is complexity in orchestration and operations: microservices need robust network communication, discovery, centralized logging, and handling of partial failures (one service down shouldn’t take down the whole system). They also introduce eventual consistency issues that a monolith (with a single database) might avoid. Many teams start monolithic for speed, and then refactor into microservices as the product and team mature – this avoids premature complexity. The decision should consider team structure (Conway’s law suggests aligning services to team boundaries), performance requirements, and tolerance for operational overhead.',
        'keywords': ['monolith', 'microservices', 'architecture', 'modularity', 'scalability', 'deployment', 'trade-offs'],
        'category': 'General',
    },
